ents{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LautaroPetaccio","id":1120791,"login":"LautaroPetaccio","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExMjA3OTE=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/commits/e6de733e41d2ba3ca512edbcffc9b01268956894/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T15:00:22Z","email":"1120791+LautaroPetaccio@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Lautaro Petaccio"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T15:00:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: give each deploy path its own concurrency group (#877)\n\n* fix: give each deploy path its own concurrency group\n\nOne group for the whole workflow meant a release and a push could never run at\nthe same time, and GitHub keeps only one pending run per group: a release\nwaiting behind a push deploy was cancelled outright when the next push landed.\nA cancelled run deploys nothing and reports no failure, which is the failure\nthis migration is most prone to.\n\nSerialising them here was never what protected the KV record. The broker takes\na lease per environment and record before it writes, and answers\n`rollout_in_progress` with a Retry-After that the action already treats as\nretryable, so two deploys reaching the write at once resolve on their own.\n\nPushes keep a shared group, where a newer commit superseding a pending older\none is the wanted behaviour. Releases are keyed by tag and promotions by\nenvironment, so neither can cancel the other.\n\n* fix: describe the rollback this job can actually perform\n\nThe comment said a rollback needs the abandoned version set to 0%, \"which this\nsingle-version dispatch cannot express\", and sent the reader to\nset-rollout-manual.\n\nBoth halves are wrong. `percentage` accepts 0 in the action and in the broker,\nso this job can zero a record. What one dispatch cannot do is name two versions,\nand a rollback changes two: the abandoned version down to 0% and the one being\nrestored back to 100%. So it is this job twice, not a different workflow -- and\nset-rollout-manual writes KV through the relay this replaces, outside the\nbroker''s lease.\n\n* fix: describe a rollback that actually restores the target\n\nThe previous wording said a rollback is two dispatches, the abandoned version\nto 0% and the target to 100%. That holds only when exactly one record sits\nabove the target. Records are sorted descending by semver and the worker serves\nthe first whose percentage covers the user, so every version above the target\nhas to be zeroed -- on zone that is usually several commit builds.\n\nIt also had the order backwards. Zeroing first leaves a window where whatever\nrecord sits below the target serves instead, and these dispatches share a\nconcurrency group per environment, so a third queued behind a running one\ncancels the second while it is pending -- deploying nothing and reporting no\nfailure, which is the shape this workflow exists to avoid. Restoring the target\nfirst and zeroing downward never opens that window.\n\nIt dropped the pointer to `set-rollout-manual.yml`, which is still the only way\nto restore a version published before this workflow existed: those prefixes\ncarry no `.deploy-complete.json`, and the broker refuses to point at an upload\nit cannot confirm finished.\n\nThe `dev` comment claimed the broker serialises the KV write. It serialises\nwrites made through the broker; `set-rollout-manual.yml` goes through\nwebhooks-receiver, which takes no lease, so the two can still lose an update\nagainst each other.\n\n* docs: name the version a bad rollback order would serve\n\nThe comment said zeroing first \"opens a gap where a record below the target\nserves\". `calculateRollout` is more specific than that: it skips records at 0%\nand, when no record covers the user, returns the LAST one -- which, with records\nsorted descending, is the oldest version still stored.\n\nSo the cost of the wrong order is not the next version down, it is the oldest\none on record, and it lasts until the second dispatch lands. On org that waits\non the environment approval gate.\n\nRestoring first remains invisible, since a newer record still sorts ahead and\nkeeps serving, which is what makes the safe order free.","tree":{"sha":"24fc1c0a300073d10a91dad2554b2580bc34529f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/git/trees/24fc1c0a300073d10a91dad2554b2580bc34529f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/git/commits/e6de733e41d2ba3ca512edbcffc9b01268956894","verification":{"payload":"tree 24fc1c0a300073d10a91dad2554b2580bc34529f\nparent 54e2e509cec854a69dc9454494beeda2fc5d69b8\nauthor Lautaro Petaccio <1120791+LautaroPetaccio@users.noreply.github.com> 1790866822 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790866822 -0300\n\nfix: give each deploy path its own concurrency group (#877)\n\n* fix: give each deploy path its own concurrency group\n\nOne group for the whole workflow meant a release and a push could never run at\nthe same time, and GitHub keeps only one pending run per group: a release\nwaiting behind a push deploy was cancelled outright when the next push landed.\nA cancelled run deploys nothing and reports no failure, which is the failure\nthis migration is most prone to.\n\nSerialising them here was never what protected the KV record. The broker takes\na lease per environment and record before it writes, and answers\n`rollout_in_progress` with a Retry-After that the action already treats as\nretryable, so two deploys reaching the write at once resolve on their own.\n\nPushes keep a shared group, where a newer commit superseding a pending older\none is the wanted behaviour. Releases are keyed by tag and promotions by\nenvironment, so neither can cancel the other.\n\n* fix: describe the rollback this job can actually perform\n\nThe comment said a rollback needs the abandoned version set to 0%, \"which this\nsingle-version dispatch cannot express\", and sent the reader to\nset-rollout-manual.\n\nBoth halves are wrong. `percentage` accepts 0 in the action and in the broker,\nso this job can zero a record. What one dispatch cannot do is name two versions,\nand a rollback changes two: the abandoned version down to 0% and the one being\nrestored back to 100%. So it is this job twice, not a different workflow -- and\nset-rollout-manual writes KV through the relay this replaces, outside the\nbroker''s lease.\n\n* fix: describe a rollback that actually restores the target\n\nThe previous wording said a rollback is two dispatches, the abandoned version\nto 0% and the target to 100%. That holds only when exactly one record sits\nabove the target. Records are sorted descending by semver and the worker serves\nthe first whose percentage covers the user, so every version above the target\nhas to be zeroed -- on zone that is usually several commit builds.\n\nIt also had the order backwards. Zeroing first leaves a window where whatever\nrecord sits below the target serves instead, and these dispatches share a\nconcurrency group per environment, so a third queued behind a running one\ncancels the second while it is pending -- deploying nothing and reporting no\nfailure, which is the shape this workflow exists to avoid. Restoring the target\nfirst and zeroing downward never opens that window.\n\nIt dropped the pointer to `set-rollout-manual.yml`, which is still the only way\nto restore a version published before this workflow existed: those prefixes\ncarry no `.deploy-complete.json`, and the broker refuses to point at an upload\nit cannot confirm finished.\n\nThe `dev` comment claimed the broker serialises the KV write. It serialises\nwrites made through the broker; `set-rollout-manual.yml` goes through\nwebhooks-receiver, which takes no lease, so the two can still lose an update\nagainst each other.\n\n* docs: name the version a bad rollback order would serve\n\nThe comment said zeroing first \"opens a gap where a record below the target\nserves\". `calculateRollout` is more specific than that: it skips records at 0%\nand, when no record covers the user, returns the LAST one -- which, with records\nsorted descending, is the oldest version still stored.\n\nSo the cost of the wrong order is not the next version down, it is the oldest\none on record, and it lasts until the second dispatch lands. On org that waits\non the environment approval gate.\n\nRestoring first remains invisible, since a newer record still sorts ahead and\nkeeps serving, which is what makes the safe order free.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvnWGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxDAQABY7MXp0exBXS9h/SNgXGnVk\njFqWMnxyX1TJJ4f4SYQjNeh1gbIozmtmsYCC9mCxkXwNC4DxSrf/2SjpNQXqbG3N\niqpF9C0R+oaD6j4Qd0MCI2XXnqgUP1tgkn+7w3M1kqp0Rf5wANZUkXLLg7ZnfaYF\nO7T/WWXGdmaTncQ1+rxRDFRdq3eyP7oG5H2+a6xMKx3PwpG84W/fwfNKs8UAUpAA\nqT9QExovv2DSguZsBL5QOQIq50/sJ0y1eFBhQc2qx5hLAWq8UNu0kpjoPv8/h7T6\n5zrmDT6yBXS8na0wO2GVpnNf8O765TyPJ9rbXkIycYhqHHGNdoAulrxzAbTtjK8I\nPx/JHKu4o07BmC1RN8dm0bq8BFVhxF5PwhjL/F28LdR/qRuxZ30uJpTfso8QXgc9\nxLJzPBmVGcPh0uisZECwAN+z9VgWWlrmmuTaGnVQ1ijcKsE9X7RcaCCJelNiNELJ\nrzoT7Jt9vUV8d9fhuQNqzRUQXesPQGUgAoU4M0bj+Q19EroY0tAmbEELC6O7tZ+V\nabt84708z20DWFcYYauusGTpBurmYJnxvUmB8ogrkZvDey9RV/omW6BNQk+EgyJ5\nz1eSWYX3z56PalTzaH7