s/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/decentraland/sites/commit/0fcdb649010f2fdb6340d408c8f4024a6fe822bc","node_id":"C_kwDOQVSgz9oAKDBmY2RiNjQ5MDEwZjJmZGI2MzQwZDQwOGM4ZjQwMjRhNmZlODIyYmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/decentraland/sites/commit/befea65c93458dcc66e2393f0a4f258a42ed9378","sha":"befea65c93458dcc66e2393f0a4f258a42ed9378","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/commits/befea65c93458dcc66e2393f0a4f258a42ed9378"}],"sha":"0fcdb649010f2fdb6340d408c8f4024a6fe822bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/commits/0fcdb649010f2fdb6340d408c8f4024a6fe822bc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1120791?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LautaroPetaccio","id":1120791,"login":"LautaroPetaccio","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExMjA3OTE=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LautaroPetaccio","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/commits/befea65c93458dcc66e2393f0a4f258a42ed9378/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-11T17:54:14Z","email":"1120791+LautaroPetaccio@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Lautaro Petaccio"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-11T17:54:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: migrate community reads to the social-service v2 endpoints (#751)\n\n* feat: migrate community reads to the social-service v2 endpoints\n\nThe v2 community read endpoints return addresses only, skipping the\nCatalyst profile enrichment v1 does server-side. Point the single\ncommunity, members, member-requests, communities-list and discover\ncommunities-list reads at them and resolve the display fields\nclient-side: useProfileAvatar for the owner row, one batched useProfiles\nlookup per members page.\n\nWrites and /v1/members/:address/communities have no v2 counterpart and\nstay on v1.\n\nLayer a requested-address guard on top of the batch profile parse: both\nuserId and ethAddress are deployer-controlled, so an avatar claiming an\naddress the batch did not ask for is dropped rather than filling that\nmember''s row.\n\n* fix: hold the members skeleton until the profile batch resolves\n\nAddress-only members render before their Catalyst batch lands, so the\nlist flashed raw hex addresses where v1 went straight from skeleton to\nnamed rows.\n\nMove the enrichment into useCommunityMemberCards, which gates the list\nuntil the batch covers the members on screen. Coverage, not emptiness,\nis the test: the profile cache is shared, so it can already hold entries\nfor a community being switched into. Once a community has resolved once,\nan appended page holds back only its own pending rows instead of\nblanking the list. A member with no deployed profile still gets a cache\nentry, so nobody is hidden and the address fallback stands.\n\nAlso type the card role as Role instead of string, which caught two spec\nfixtures using an \"admin\" role this endpoint never returns.\n\n* refactor: serve every profile read from a single cache\n\nuseProfiles kept a second hand-rolled cache in features/cast2/peer.ts,\nso the community detail resolved its owner through profile.client and\nits members through peer.ts — two caches, two requests for the same\naddress.\n\nPoint useProfiles at profile.client, which since #493 already batches\nevery read in a tick into one POST /lambdas/profiles per peer, and\ndelete peer.ts. Its public shape is unchanged, so cast and discover\nconsumers keep working, and the owner row now shares a batch with the\nmember list instead of being fetched twice.\n\nTwo additions make the store cover what peer.ts did: an optional peer\nURL per call, which cast and discover rely on, and a hasError flag so a\nfailed batch stops callers waiting instead of spinning forever. The\naddress-claim guard is no longer needed — the store settles each\nrequested address from the response rather than mapping the response\nonto rows.\n\n* fix: drop contested profile claims from a batch response\n\nThe batch profile response has no trusted binding: content-validator\nties a profile entity''s POINTER to the signer but never the ethAddress\nwritten inside the metadata, and lambdas spreads that metadata through\nuntouched. Keying rows off that field let one profile answer for\nanother address requested in the same batch.\n\nIgnore claims on addresses the batch did not ask for, and drop any row\ntwo entries claim, so a contested row falls back to its address instead\nof rendering a stolen name and avatar.\n\nAlso track resolved communities in a Set rather than the last id, so\nreturning to an earlier community does not read as unresolved, and give\nthe PeopleStack fixtures a real address instead of casting past the\ntype.\n\n* docs: correct the trust note on the batched profile bind\n\nlamb2 4.13.2 pins userId/ethAddress to the entity pointer and\ncontent-validator rejects mismatched deployments, so the claimed address\nis trustworthy against an up-to-date peer. The earlier note described\nthe legacy catalyst lambdas package, which no longer serves prod.\n\nKeep the bind as defence in depth: the response carries no pointer to\ncheck against and PEER_URL can point at an older peer.\n\n* fix: clear the profile error flag while a retry is in flight\n\nA failed batch left hasError on the entry, and starting the retry spread\nthat entry forward, so snapshots reported errored-not-loading until the\nretry finished. Callers stayed on the address fallback instead of\nshowing a spinner.\n\nClear the flag alongside the new in-flight promise.\n\n* fix: settle failed profile batches and skeleton the community rows\n\nAddresses the review on #751.\n\n- useProfiles keeps an (empty) entry for an address whose batch failed, so\n  `profiles.has()` means \"settled\" again and the cast chat shows the short\n  address instead of \"…\" after a failure.\n- The batched POST /lambdas/profiles gets a 10s timeout, and a 200 whose\n  body is not a list settles as a failed batch instead of being cached as\n  \"nobody here has a profile\". The no-op requested-address filter is gone.\n- Member rows render as soon as their page lands, with per-row skeletons\n  (AvatarSkeleton / NameSkeleton, shared with the owner row). The list-level\n  gate, its resolved-communities ref and the early-firing sentinel go away.\n- The owner row holds a skeleton while its profile loads and falls back to\n  the truncated address instead of \"Unknown\"; the orphaned i18n key is\n  removed from all six locales.\n- communities.client.spec uses a strict response queue, resets mocks,\n  drains RTK refetches after each case and holds the post-rejection refetch\n  open so the undo cases no longer pass on timing.\n\n* fix: truncate the member row address fallback\n\nA member with no profile (and, after a failed batch, every member) showed the\nfull 42-character address. It is one unbreakable token, so it overflowed the\nfixed 320px members column and MembersList''s overflow-x hidden clipped it\nmid-string. Use truncateAddress, the same fallback the owner row and the rest\nof the profile surfaces already use; the card keeps the full address, which\nkeys the row and seeds its avatar colour.\n\nThe specs used 5-character stubs, which truncateAddress passes through\nunchanged, so they never exercised this path. They now use full-length\naddresses.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Braian Mellor <braianj@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"235c4fb1bce5354d811b1a3a246f545708f65124","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/git/trees/235c4fb1bce5354d811b1a3a246f545708f65124"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/decentraland/sites/git/commits/befea65c93458dcc66e2393f0a4f258a42ed9378","verification":{"payload":"tree 235c4fb1bce5354d811b1a3a246f545708f65124\nparent 7308c57757e0fdd6824c1e12f94270339c65631c\nauthor Lautaro Petaccio <1120791+LautaroPetaccio@users.noreply.github.com> 1789149254 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789149254 -0300\n\nfeat: migrate community reads to the social-service v2 endpoints (#751)\n\n* feat: migrate community reads to the social-service v2 endpoints\n\nThe v2 community read endpoints return addresses only, skipping the\nCatalyst profile enrichment v1 does server-side. Point the single\ncommunity, members, member-requests, communities-list and discover\ncommunities-list reads at them and resolve the display fields\nclient-side: useProfileAvatar for the owner row, one batched useProfiles\nlookup per members page.\n\nWrites and /v1/members/:address/communities have no v2 counterpart and\nstay on v1.\n\nLayer a requested-address guard on top of the batch profile parse: both\nuserId and ethAddress are deployer-controlled, so an avatar claiming an\naddress the batch did not ask for is dropped rather than filling that\nmember''s row.\n\n* fix: hold the members skeleton until the profile batch resolves\n\nAddress-only members render before their Catalyst batch lands, so the\nlist flashed raw hex addresses where v1 went straight from skeleton to\nnamed rows.\n\nMove the enrichment into useCommunityMemberCards, which gates the list\nuntil the batch covers the members on screen. Coverage, not emptiness,\nis the test: the profile cache is shared, so it can already hold entries\nfor a community being switched into. Once a community has resolved once,\nan appended page holds back only its own pending rows instead of\nblanking the list. A member with no deployed profile still gets a cache\nentry, so nobody is hidden and the address fallback stands.\n\nAlso type the card role as Role instead of string, which caught two spec\nfixtures using an \"admin\" role this endpoint never returns.\n\n* refactor: serve every profile read from a single cache\n\nuseProfiles kept a second hand-rolled cache in features/cast2/peer.ts,\nso the community detail resolved its owner through profile.client and\nits members through peer.ts — two caches, two requests for the same\naddress.\n\nPoint useProfiles at profile.client, which since #493 already batches\nevery read in a tick into one POST /lambdas/profiles per peer, and\ndelete peer.ts. Its public shape is unchanged, so cast and discover\nconsumers keep working, and the owner row now shares a batch with the\nmember list instead of being fetched twice.\n\nTwo additions make the store cover what peer.ts did: an optional peer\nURL per call, which cast and discover rely on, and a hasError flag so a\nfailed batch stops callers waiting instead of spinning forever. The\naddress-claim guard is no longer needed — the store settles each\nrequested address from the response rather than mapping the response\nonto rows.\n\n* fix: drop contested profile claims from a batch response\n\nThe batch profile response has no trusted binding: content-validator\nties a profile entity''s POINTER to the signer but never the ethAddress\nwritten inside the metadata, and lambdas spreads that metadata through\nuntouched. Keying rows off that field let one profile answer for\nanother address requested in the same batch.\n\nIgnore claims on addresses the batch did not ask for, and drop any row\ntwo entries claim, so a contested row falls back to its address instead\nof rendering a stolen name and avatar.\n\nAlso track resolved communities in a Set rather than the last id, so\nreturning to an earlier community does not read as unresolved, and give\nthe PeopleStack fixtures a real address instead of casting past the\ntype.\n\n* docs: correct the trust note on the batched profile bind\n\nlamb2 4.13.2 pins userId/ethAddress to the entity pointer and\ncontent-validator rejects mismatched deployments, so the claimed address\nis trustworthy against an up-to-date peer. The earlier note described\nthe legacy catalyst lambdas package, which no longer serves prod.\n\nKeep the bind as defence in depth: the response carries no pointer to\ncheck against and PEER_URL can point at an older peer.\n\n* fix: clear the profile error flag while a retry is in flight\n\nA failed batch left hasError on the entry, and starting the retry spread\nthat entry forward, so snapshots reported errored-not-loading until the\nretry finished. Callers stayed on the address fallback instead of\nshowing a spinner.\n\nClear the flag alongside the new in-flight promise.\n\n* fix: settle failed profile batches and skeleton the community rows\n\nAddresses the review on #751.\n\n- useProfiles keeps an (empty) entry for an address whose batch failed, so\n  `profiles.has()` means \"settled\" again and the cast chat shows the short\n  address instead of \"…\" after a failure.\n- The batched POST /lambdas/profiles gets a 10s timeout, and a 200 whose\n  body is not a list settles as a failed batch instead of being cached as\n  \"nobody here has a profile\". The no-op requested-address filter is gone.\n- Member rows render as soon as their page lands, with per-row skeletons\n  (AvatarSkeleton / NameSkeleton, shared with the owner row). The list-level\n  gate, its resolved-communities ref and the early-firing sentinel go away.\n- The owner row holds a skeleton while its profile loads and falls back to\n  the truncated address instead of \"Unknown\"; the orphaned i18n key is\n  removed from all six locales.\n- communities.client.spec uses a strict response queue, resets mocks,\n  drains RTK refetches after each case and holds the post-rejection refetch\n  open so the undo cases no longer pass on timing.\n\n* fix: truncate the member row address fallback\n\nA member with no profile (and, after a failed batch, every member) showed the\nfull 42-character address. It is one unbreakable token, so it overflowed the\nfixed 320px members column and MembersList''s overflow-x hidden clipped it\nmid-string. Use truncateAddress, the same fallback the owner row and the rest\nof the profile surfaces already use; the card keeps the full address, which\nkeys the row and seeds its avatar colour.\n\nThe specs used 5-character stubs, which truncateAddress passes through\nunchanged, so they never exercised this path. They now use full-length\naddresses.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Braian Mellor <braianj@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqpEBGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAAP4QAHhwRLFLQHXXnqXu3IUWA3ln\nsSCjFVIP2lwIw6LBFNZYQ5mN1KtK5lQ/P0MDalu1PoztJJumSj5bMCh7bcM6FByG\nt/SGneu/b1lyxvjZhQGxamm29mGdlx5iyXOcogArddlUfTuI6SkZKTuytBcT91ZY\ns2kyNootx8eCvJjmX+jTNea3L3nyk7U8NPzy/eQA1a5gPsVPG+4Q9zSntTkWNYFl\nEYooO+gt6BRsmL2Yr8k/708IXlVW6fznIc/HuwBRxVU8aJkgiJOeYifyo4etSimW\nG2capB1I4nirBsSc1/gNG0yzAlEaHJTFbiWF1O8Lh7BoJ/RImyFfKSCKJIscpsnC\nKw56Ez69CW9Ot0KRhfgyjOI0mnEN/yRuNsI0QAqSuis5zCdpMSFnk4RuMl5hgyNz\nhqii9yv4dswlLCF8ahNx4ijR96AvjDlPMJ6E6Dk6wbTVk0Twt2AdBbPaGgZNAFWP\nTja8ngxKXgp282roj0MhX99mObWzhQfA0aZzSK70MxopLDZBcW7WdYmNNDlFuEgQ\nEH+aazgCMmICGyDCbpWB5GbyDGYSOtXV8/nVyp7RRtZwCbFP4N5uwySuloO4vrxn\nBdx2KYqzsGjaIojCnjJqrqo0K212XFTsHd4VFYclIUazvWOaIdPF6AILPQTle0m8\nWt/7o6NFRVAQbsujQwrz\n=Pq5s\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-11T17:54:15Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercon